          "id": "sec-1-3",
          "secNumber": "1.3",
          "heading": "Idealization, free bodies, and the cut method",
          "content": "## Lesson 1.1 \u2014 Idealization, free bodies, and the cut method\n\n### Physical mechanism and governing idea\n\nA strength-of-materials calculation starts with a load path, not with a stress formula. A structure transfers external force to supports through internal forces. At a chosen cut, equilibrium exposes those internal resultants. Normal force, shear force, torque and bending moment summarize interactions across the section. The sign convention must be defined before one writes an equation.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\n**N, V, T, M obtained from equilibrium of a cut section**\n\n**Reading the equation:** Start from a properly oriented free body or a measured constitutive response, identify the force or displacement controlled externally, and decide which geometric quantity belongs in the denominator. Check units before substituting numbers, and compare the sign of the result with the physical action.\n\n### Worked interpretive example\n\nA prismatic hanger carries 18 kN of tension. A cut through the hanger must transmit 18 kN in the opposite direction to balance its free body.\n\n### What the simplified formula misses\n\nDo not infer that an internal force is uniformly distributed merely because the net resultant is known. Distribution requires a material and geometric model.\n\n### Engineering reasoning exercise\n\n1. Explain in your own words which observable physical quantity the formula in Lesson 1.1 is estimating. Specify where it would be measured.\n2. Change exactly one of the underlying dimensions or loading parameters, predict the direction of change before calculating, and say which other variables are held constant.\n3. Invent a connection, defect or boundary condition under which the approximation in this lesson becomes unreliable. Suggest the simplest more faithful model.\n4. State a dimensional consistency check and one limiting case that should hold if the stated equations are applied correctly."
