          "heading": "Mechanical properties and safety factors",
          "content": "## Lesson 1.4 \u2014 Mechanical properties and safety factors\n\n### Physical mechanism and governing idea\n\nA constitutive law connects stress and strain. The linear elastic relation sigma=E epsilon is a limited approximation that applies only over the stated loading range. Yield strength concerns irreversible material response, whereas fracture strength concerns separation. A factor of safety is a design convention applied to a particular failure mode and should not be mistaken for a guarantee.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\n**sigma = E epsilon within the linear elastic range; FoS = reference resistance / demand**\n\n**Reading the equation:** Start from a properly oriented free body or a measured constitutive response, identify the force or displacement controlled externally, and decide which geometric quantity belongs in the denominator. Check units before substituting numbers, and compare the sign of the result with the physical action.\n\n### Worked interpretive example\n\nFor a nominal 250 MPa yield strength and 100 MPa elastic normal stress, a simple yield-ratio screen gives 2.5.\n\n### What the simplified formula misses\n\nA ratio calculated for yielding does not automatically address fatigue, instability, brittle fracture, corrosion, joining details or applicable design codes.\n\n### Engineering reasoning exercise\n\n1. Explain in your own words which observable physical quantity the formula in Lesson 1.4 is estimating. Specify where it would be measured.\n2. Change exactly one of the underlying dimensions or loading parameters, predict the direction of change before calculating, and say which other variables are held constant.\n3. Invent a connection, defect or boundary condition under which the approximation in this lesson becomes unreliable. Suggest the simplest more faithful model.\n4. State a dimensional consistency check and one limiting case that should hold if the stated equations are applied correctly."
