          "heading": "Engineering case study: Bridge inspection: load path through a corroded gusset",
          "content": "## Engineering case study: Bridge inspection: load path through a corroded gusset\n\nAn inspection crew observes local section loss around a hole in a steel connection plate. A uniform-force calculation based on gross plate area underestimates the local force intensity. The first useful step is to map the force path from the attached member into the connection through bolts and bearing faces. Then distinguish gross-section yield, net-section rupture, bearing, bolt shear and block-shear failure as separate mechanisms. Measure the effective thickness and geometry and identify which original drawing dimensions remain trustworthy.\n\n**Deliverable:** Report the applied member force, the gross and net areas, estimated average stresses, and a list of additional details needed to apply the appropriate steel design specification. Ask whether corrosion is localized or uniform and how the chosen assumptions change the computed resistance.\n\n### Case-study discussion\n\nStart by differentiating data from assumptions: measured loads and geometry should be separated from guessed restraints and material properties. Build one simple calculation whose result can be checked independently. List at least three mechanisms by which the idealization could fail, and prioritize them by whether they would change the engineering conclusion. Translate the numerical demand into a practical question about safety, serviceability, testing or maintenance. Keep a clear audit trail so a reviewer can follow the unit conversions, load path and model limitations."
