          "content": "## Unit context and engineering interpretation\n\nAxial behavior provides a clean setting in which equilibrium, compatibility and constitutive response must work together. If two members lie end to end, the same axial force passes through each segment, assuming no additional load enters between them; their extensions add. If two rods share rigid end plates, their total extensions are equal but the forces differ according to EA/L. A rigid support can attract an internal reaction in the absence of any external mechanical load when temperature or fabrication prestrain is imposed. These observations are not separate tricks: each is a consequence of the same force-displacement law combined with mechanical constraints.\n\nSuppose an anchored steel element experiences a prescribed temperature rise. The unconstrained thermal strain is alpha \u0394T, whereas actual mechanical strain is the difference between total strain and thermal strain. Under perfect restraint, total axial strain vanishes, so a compressive stress develops. If one end is attached to a flexible spring, part of the free expansion occurs as actual movement and the remaining part develops stress. This intermediate case is common in pipe supports and clamped equipment. A fully fixed model is sometimes an upper bound on force, but not always on local stress once geometry, contact and eccentricity enter.\n\nStored energy offers an independent perspective. A uniform elastic bar has a triangular force-displacement diagram, so its strain energy equals one half of final force times extension. The relation is not generally correct for a nonlinear material unless the actual area under the curve is computed. If the stiffness doubles while force remains fixed, deflection and strain energy halve. If instead a prescribed displacement is applied, force and strain energy both increase with stiffness. The chosen control variable matters."
