          "content": "## Engineering case study: Thermally restrained pipeline support\n\nA heated water pipe runs between anchors but incorporates expansion loops and flexible supports. Under a purely fixed bar approximation, the thermal stress is proportional to E alpha deltaT and does not depend on pipe length. Under a free expansion approximation, the displacement is proportional to length. Neither extreme alone describes a pipe with spring supports and sliding friction. The engineer must characterize anchor stiffness and temperature distribution along the line.\n\n**Deliverable:** Construct a three-case comparison: free expansion, full restraint and a finite axial restraint spring. Identify where compatibility enters the model and which instrumentation could measure anchor movement and actual axial strain.\n\n### Case-study discussion\n\nStart by differentiating data from assumptions: measured loads and geometry should be separated from guessed restraints and material properties. Build one simple calculation whose result can be checked independently. List at least three mechanisms by which the idealization could fail, and prioritize them by whether they would change the engineering conclusion. Translate the numerical demand into a practical question about safety, serviceability, testing or maintenance. Keep a clear audit trail so a reviewer can follow the unit conversions, load path and model limitations."
