          "heading": "Poisson ratio and multiaxial elastic response",
          "content": "## Lesson 3.2 \u2014 Poisson ratio and multiaxial elastic response\n\n### Physical mechanism and governing idea\n\nAn axially stretched isotropic linear elastic specimen generally contracts laterally. The Poisson ratio relates transverse strain to axial strain under uniaxial stress. In three-dimensional elasticity, strain depends on all three normal stresses. Material properties must be assessed for the appropriate direction in composites and other anisotropic solids.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\n**epsilon_trans = -nu epsilon_axial; epsilon_x = (sigma_x - nu(sigma_y + sigma_z))/E**\n\n**Reading the equation:** Start from a properly oriented free body or a measured constitutive response, identify the force or displacement controlled externally, and decide which geometric quantity belongs in the denominator. Check units before substituting numbers, and compare the sign of the result with the physical action.\n\n### Worked interpretive example\n\nAn axial strain of 900 microstrain and nu=0.30 predicts a transverse strain of -270 microstrain in uniaxial linear elasticity.\n\n### What the simplified formula misses\n\nThe Poisson formula alone is not sufficient for a plane-strain constraint or a layered material that responds differently in different directions.\n\n### Engineering reasoning exercise\n\n1. Explain in your own words which observable physical quantity the formula in Lesson 3.2 is estimating. Specify where it would be measured.\n2. Change exactly one of the underlying dimensions or loading parameters, predict the direction of change before calculating, and say which other variables are held constant.\n3. Invent a connection, defect or boundary condition under which the approximation in this lesson becomes unreliable. Suggest the simplest more faithful model.\n4. State a dimensional consistency check and one limiting case that should hold if the stated equations are applied correctly."
