          "content": "## Engineering case study: Material substitution for a small lifting bracket\n\nA project team considers replacing a steel component with aluminum to reduce mass. The two candidate materials may have different yield strengths, moduli, density and fatigue behavior. Equal dimensions do not imply equal stiffness or equal safety. If applied load is prescribed, elastic extension scales inversely with E; if assembly displacement is prescribed, reactions can increase with stiffness.\n\n**Deliverable:** Create a decision matrix separating stiffness, strength, deformation limit, corrosion exposure, joining, cost and standard-compliant inspection. Do not assign a single winner until required limits and the actual load control method are known.\n\n### Case-study discussion\n\nStart by differentiating data from assumptions: measured loads and geometry should be separated from guessed restraints and material properties. Build one simple calculation whose result can be checked independently. List at least three mechanisms by which the idealization could fail, and prioritize them by whether they would change the engineering conclusion. Translate the numerical demand into a practical question about safety, serviceability, testing or maintenance. Keep a clear audit trail so a reviewer can follow the unit conversions, load path and model limitations."
