          "content": "## Structured unit assessment\n\nAnswer the following without access to the provided worked solutions. Each question needs a diagram, assumptions and a clear interpretation.\n\n1. Use a load or material value from Case 4.02, change one geometric dimension by 20%, and calculate the resulting response with explicit units.\n2. Explain how the result of Lesson 4.1 would change if one apparently rigid support had finite stiffness.\n3. Choose a nonuniform or damaged member for which the idealization in Lesson 4.2 becomes inaccurate; describe a better analytical or experimental representation.\n4. Derive a useful limiting case from the relationship in Lesson 4.3 and compare with a limiting setting in any associated simulation.\n5. A reviewer reports a one-thousandfold discrepancy. Show one plausible N\u2013kN, m\u2013mm or N\u00b7m\u2013N\u00b7mm conversion error that could create it for a calculation in this unit.\n6. For the case study in Unit 4, distinguish two possible failure modes and explain why demonstrating adequate performance against one does not guarantee the other.\n7. Create an experimental plan to validate one of the numerical predictions in Unit 4; name the sensor, its calibration requirement and an expected source of uncertainty.\n8. State two ways that manufacturing tolerance, support flexibility or temperature could alter a result computed from one equation in Unit 4.\n\n### Associated interactive laboratories\n\n**Laboratory 03: Circular Shaft Torsion.**\nWithin elastic Saint-Venant torsion theory, shear stress increases from zero at the center to the outside surface. Changing diameter alters J dramatically. Examine how diameter affects both peak stress and twist.\n\n**Investigation:** Double diameter while holding torque and length constant. Estimate the reduction in stress before observing the numerical outputs.\n\nOpen the [working HTML model](../labs/lab-03-torsion.html), record inputs and CSV data, and compare numerical outputs with an independent calculation. Identify the limiting assumptions before discussing how a real physical experiment would differ."
