          "id": "sec-5-6",
          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Load envelopes and structural reasoning",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.4 \u2014 Load envelopes and structural reasoning\n\n### Physical mechanism and governing idea\n\nReal beams experience multiple load combinations and moving load positions. A single nominal diagram is only one scenario. Load envelopes capture maximum and minimum internal demands across specified combinations, while code factors and pattern loading remain separate decisions. A safe design begins with correct demand definitions and relevant limit states.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\n**M_envelope(x)=max over admissible load cases of |M_case(x)|**\n\n**Reading the equation:** Start from a properly oriented free body or a measured constitutive response, identify the force or displacement controlled externally, and decide which geometric quantity belongs in the denominator. Check units before substituting numbers, and compare the sign of the result with the physical action.\n\n### Worked interpretive example\n\nMoving a concentrated load toward a support reduces its maximum simple-span moment even when the load magnitude is unchanged.\n\n### What the simplified formula misses\n\nAn envelope over only the cases you chose says nothing about omitted traffic positions, construction stages or redistribution due to cracking.\n\n### Engineering reasoning exercise\n\n1. Explain in your own words which observable physical quantity the formula in Lesson 5.4 is estimating. Specify where it would be measured.\n2. Change exactly one of the underlying dimensions or loading parameters, predict the direction of change before calculating, and say which other variables are held constant.\n3. Invent a connection, defect or boundary condition under which the approximation in this lesson becomes unreliable. Suggest the simplest more faithful model.\n4. State a dimensional consistency check and one limiting case that should hold if the stated equations are applied correctly."
