          "heading": "Engineering case study: Temporary construction loading on a floor beam",
          "content": "## Engineering case study: Temporary construction loading on a floor beam\n\nDuring renovation, material pallets are placed close to one end of a simply supported beam. A uniform load approximation may miss concentrated reaction and moment peaks. The engineer should represent the actual pallet footprint or applied point-force idealization, solve support reactions and plot piecewise shear and bending moments. Repositioning the load changes support demands and bending behavior even when total weight remains unchanged.\n\n**Deliverable:** Develop two loading cases: one centered and one near a support. Compare reactions, maximum moment location and the shear jump. State why this demonstration cannot replace a building-code load combination and restraint investigation.\n\n### Case-study discussion\n\nStart by differentiating data from assumptions: measured loads and geometry should be separated from guessed restraints and material properties. Build one simple calculation whose result can be checked independently. List at least three mechanisms by which the idealization could fail, and prioritize them by whether they would change the engineering conclusion. Translate the numerical demand into a practical question about safety, serviceability, testing or maintenance. Keep a clear audit trail so a reviewer can follow the unit conversions, load path and model limitations."
