          "heading": "Engineering case study: Deflection-sensitive laboratory equipment platform",
          "content": "## Engineering case study: Deflection-sensitive laboratory equipment platform\n\nA steel supporting beam meets an initial bending stress target, yet an optical instrument suffers vibration and alignment errors. Increasing section modulus can help strength; increasing EI helps static deflection. Natural frequencies and dynamic excitation require an additional dynamics model. Floor finishes, partition details and instrument requirements may impose serviceability limits stricter than generic rule-of-thumb span limits.\n\n**Deliverable:** Compute static midpoint deflection for point and distributed loads, compare different member depths, and identify a suitable measured acceptance criterion. Explain why elastic static deflection alone cannot establish satisfactory vibration performance.\n\n### Case-study discussion\n\nStart by differentiating data from assumptions: measured loads and geometry should be separated from guessed restraints and material properties. Build one simple calculation whose result can be checked independently. List at least three mechanisms by which the idealization could fail, and prioritize them by whether they would change the engineering conclusion. Translate the numerical demand into a practical question about safety, serviceability, testing or maintenance. Keep a clear audit trail so a reviewer can follow the unit conversions, load path and model limitations."
