          "content": "## Structured unit assessment\n\nAnswer the following without access to the provided worked solutions. Each question needs a diagram, assumptions and a clear interpretation.\n\n1. Use a load or material value from Case 7.02, change one geometric dimension by 20%, and calculate the resulting response with explicit units.\n2. Explain how the result of Lesson 7.1 would change if one apparently rigid support had finite stiffness.\n3. Choose a nonuniform or damaged member for which the idealization in Lesson 7.2 becomes inaccurate; describe a better analytical or experimental representation.\n4. Derive a useful limiting case from the relationship in Lesson 7.3 and compare with a limiting setting in any associated simulation.\n5. A reviewer reports a one-thousandfold discrepancy. Show one plausible N\u2013kN, m\u2013mm or N\u00b7m\u2013N\u00b7mm conversion error that could create it for a calculation in this unit.\n6. For the case study in Unit 7, distinguish two possible failure modes and explain why demonstrating adequate performance against one does not guarantee the other.\n7. Create an experimental plan to validate one of the numerical predictions in Unit 7; name the sensor, its calibration requirement and an expected source of uncertainty.\n8. State two ways that manufacturing tolerance, support flexibility or temperature could alter a result computed from one equation in Unit 7.\n\n### Associated interactive laboratories\n\n**Laboratory 07: Mohr\u2019s Circle and Stress Rotation.**\nNormal and shear tractions transform with twice the physical angle. The circle visual maps these components, with center mean stress and radius determined by normal difference and shear. The angle control represents an oriented outward plane normal.\n\n**Investigation:** Set normal stresses equal and shear to zero. Observe the degenerate circle. Increase shear; predict the changes in principal stresses and principal direction.\n\nOpen the [working HTML model](../labs/lab-07-mohr.html), record inputs and CSV data, and compare numerical outputs with an independent calculation. Identify the limiting assumptions before discussing how a real physical experiment would differ.\n\n**Laboratory 08: Combined Axial + Bending Stress.**\nAxial stress N/A adds to an ideal linear bending distribution M y/I. The result can change sign across a member; the neutral axis moves when axial load is applied. The outer fibers carry the largest absolute bending contribution.\n\n**Investigation:** At zero axial load, compare the top and bottom extreme stresses. Apply tensile axial load until one edge becomes entirely tensile, then explain the shift.\n\nOpen the [working HTML model](../labs/lab-08-combined.html), record inputs and CSV data, and compare numerical outputs with an independent calculation. Identify the limiting assumptions before discussing how a real physical experiment would differ."
