          "secNumber": "9.6",
          "heading": "Verification, uncertainty and responsible use",
          "content": "## Lesson 9.4 \u2014 Verification, uncertainty and responsible use\n\n### Physical mechanism and governing idea\n\nEngineering judgment includes model verification, validation and uncertainty management. Verification asks whether equations and software were implemented correctly; validation asks whether the equations represent the real physical system. A robust design record identifies input assumptions, load combinations, manufacturing tolerances and independent checks.\n\n### Governing relationship\n\n**Compare independent equilibrium checks, dimensions, limiting cases and measured behavior**\n\n**Reading the equation:** Start from a properly oriented free body or a measured constitutive response, identify the force or displacement controlled externally, and decide which geometric quantity belongs in the denominator. Check units before substituting numbers, and compare the sign of the result with the physical action.\n\n### Worked interpretive example\n\nA beam simulation may pass unit tests and still misrepresent a real beam with a loose support or nonprismatic section.\n\n### What the simplified formula misses\n\nThese educational models are not licensed structural calculations. Design decisions must follow local codes, qualified review and appropriate physical testing.\n\n### Engineering reasoning exercise\n\n1. Explain in your own words which observable physical quantity the formula in Lesson 9.4 is estimating. Specify where it would be measured.\n2. Change exactly one of the underlying dimensions or loading parameters, predict the direction of change before calculating, and say which other variables are held constant.\n3. Invent a connection, defect or boundary condition under which the approximation in this lesson becomes unreliable. Suggest the simplest more faithful model.\n4. State a dimensional consistency check and one limiting case that should hold if the stated equations are applied correctly."
