          "secNumber": "9.8",
          "heading": "Engineering case study: Fatigue-sensitive vessel support under cycling pressure",
          "content": "## Engineering case study: Fatigue-sensitive vessel support under cycling pressure\n\nA small cylindrical vessel experiences repeated filling and draining. Thin-wall pressure equations explain the two membrane stresses but do not model the nozzle, weld toe or support attachment where cyclic cracking may initiate. A synthetic S-N line can demonstrate how life changes with stress amplitude, but it cannot certify the vessel. The actual load cycle includes pressure, temperature and perhaps vibration.\n\n**Deliverable:** Separate mean and alternating stresses, explain why stress concentration and inspection access matter, identify a governing pressure code and propose a validation plan involving design calculations and nondestructive examination.\n\n### Case-study discussion\n\nStart by differentiating data from assumptions: measured loads and geometry should be separated from guessed restraints and material properties. Build one simple calculation whose result can be checked independently. List at least three mechanisms by which the idealization could fail, and prioritize them by whether they would change the engineering conclusion. Translate the numerical demand into a practical question about safety, serviceability, testing or maintenance. Keep a clear audit trail so a reviewer can follow the unit conversions, load path and model limitations."
