          "content": "## Structured unit assessment\n\nAnswer the following without access to the provided worked solutions. Each question needs a diagram, assumptions and a clear interpretation.\n\n1. Use a load or material value from Case 9.02, change one geometric dimension by 20%, and calculate the resulting response with explicit units.\n2. Explain how the result of Lesson 9.1 would change if one apparently rigid support had finite stiffness.\n3. Choose a nonuniform or damaged member for which the idealization in Lesson 9.2 becomes inaccurate; describe a better analytical or experimental representation.\n4. Derive a useful limiting case from the relationship in Lesson 9.3 and compare with a limiting setting in any associated simulation.\n5. A reviewer reports a one-thousandfold discrepancy. Show one plausible N\u2013kN, m\u2013mm or N\u00b7m\u2013N\u00b7mm conversion error that could create it for a calculation in this unit.\n6. For the case study in Unit 9, distinguish two possible failure modes and explain why demonstrating adequate performance against one does not guarantee the other.\n7. Create an experimental plan to validate one of the numerical predictions in Unit 9; name the sensor, its calibration requirement and an expected source of uncertainty.\n8. State two ways that manufacturing tolerance, support flexibility or temperature could alter a result computed from one equation in Unit 9.\n\n### Associated interactive laboratories\n\n**Laboratory 10: Thin-Wall Pressure Cylinder.**\nA closed-end thin circular cylinder develops hoop stress pr/t and longitudinal stress pr/(2t). The thickness-to-radius indicator is a model applicability warning, not a certification threshold.\n\n**Investigation:** Double the radius at fixed pressure and thickness. Compare hoop and axial stress. Then make the wall relatively thick and explain why the model should not be trusted.\n\nOpen the [working HTML model](../labs/lab-10-pressure.html), record inputs and CSV data, and compare numerical outputs with an independent calculation. Identify the limiting assumptions before discussing how a real physical experiment would differ.\n\n**Laboratory 12: Fatigue S\u2013N Explorer.**\nThis synthetic log-log S\u2013N model demonstrates a fitted Basquin power relationship. It is not calibrated to any certified material and must not be used as a service-life prediction.\n\n**Investigation:** Increase stress amplitude in increments. Compare the log-scale predicted cycles. Explain why a percentage amplitude change can cause orders-of-magnitude life changes.\n\nOpen the [working HTML model](../labs/lab-12-fatigue.html), record inputs and CSV data, and compare numerical outputs with an independent calculation. Identify the limiting assumptions before discussing how a real physical experiment would differ."
